al  \n{hargov,psiden,jroll}@qti.qualcomm.com, fredrik.lindsten@liu.se\n\n## ABSTRACT\n\nThe Transformer model architecture has become one of the most widely used in deep learning and the attention mechanism is at its core. The standard attention formulation uses a softmax operation applied to a scaled dot product between query and key vectors. We explore the role played by norms of the queries and keys, which can cause training instabilities when they arbitrarily increase. We demonstrate how this can happen even in simple Transformer models, in the presence of easy-to-learn spurious patterns in the data. We propose a new attention formulation, QUery-modulated Spherical aTtention (QUEST), that constrains the keys to a hyperspherical latent space, while still allowing individual tokens to flexibly control the sharpness of the attention distribution. QUEST can be easily used as a drop-in replacement for standard attention. We focus on vision applications while also exploring other domains to highlight the method’s generality. We show that (1) QUEST trains without instabilities and (2) produces models with improved performance (3) that are robust to data corruptions and adversarial attacks.\n\n# 1 INTRODUCTION\n\nTransformers (Vaswani et al., 2017) are one of the most widely used model architectures across many domains in recent times. Each domain has adapted Transformers to build domain-specific variants such as Vision Transformers (ViT) (Dosovitskiy et al., 2020) in computer vision, GPT (Radford et al., 2018) in natural language processing, PointTransformer (Zhao et al., 2021) for 3D pointcloud data and Conformer (Gulati et al., 2020) in speech recognition, to name a few. A core building block of the Transformer that is common across such variants is the attention mechanism (Bahdanau et al., 2015; Britz et al., 2017; Luong et al., 2015). Although several different variants of the Transformer have been developed, they commonly use the vanilla attention mechanism consisting of a scaled dot-product followed by a softmax operation. Despite their success, training Transformer models can be challenging due to training instabilities (Chowdhery et al., 2023; Dehghani et al., 2023; Li et al., 2022a; Wortsman et al., 2024; Davis et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2020b; Zhai et al., 2023). Many training techniques consisting of initialization methods (Kedia et al., 2024; Huang et al., 2020), specific hyperparameter schedules (Liang et al., 2022; Kobayashi et al., 2024), normalizations (Dehghani et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2019; Xiong et al., 2020) and optimization strategies (Qi et al., 2025) have been developed to mitigate these issues. While this has limited such training instabilities to a great extent, why this issue occurs is still not fully understood (Hajra, 2025).\n\nWe study the scaled dot-product attention formulation and identify the roles of different components of this attention. We find that the arbitrary vector norms of the queries and keys may potentially cause the exploding attention logits, that is known to cause training instabilities (Zhai et al., 2023; Dehghani et al., 2023). Through a toy example, we demonstrate a scenario where the norms of these vectors increase and can lead to the model being stuck at a suboptimal solution. Even in stably trained models, we show that the model can concentrate attention on a few tokens instead of relying on all relevant tokens (see Figure 1 for an example using a ViT). We propose a new formulation, called Query-modulated Spherical Attention (QUEST), that displays improved training robustness across different hyperparameters. QUEST is a very simple modification of the standard attention and can easily be used as a drop-in replacement in any Transformer. Nevertheless, through extensive experiments--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization. (https://dblp1.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2604-00260.html)\nciteturn36240search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # \"Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic ...\" ... Phan, Jayant Kalagnanam (2026)\n\n# \"Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic ...\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nLam M. Nguyen, Dzung T. Phan, Jayant Kalagnanam (2026)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nTrier\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2604.00260\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2026-05-07\n\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n    * view\n\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2604-00260\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2604-00260\n\nLam M. Nguyen, Dzung T. Phan, Jayant Kalagnanam:\nLearning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization. CoRR abs/2604.00260 (2026)\n\n\nlast updated on 2026-05-07 09:02 CEST by the dblp team\n\n all metadata released as open data under CC0 1.0 license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications | Dat Tran (https://dattran24.github.io/Publications/)\nciteturn36240search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation ... arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.00223, 2026 ...       title = {Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation},\n# Publications\n\n\n## 2026\n\n  1. Preprint\n\nDiversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation\n\nH. C. Luong, D. B. Tran, and L. Chen\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2604.00223, 2026\n    \n    `@article{luong2026diversity,\n      title = {Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation},\n      author = {Luong, H. C. and Tran, D. B. and Chen, L.},\n      journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.00223},\n      year = {2026},\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.00223}\n\n  2. Preprint\n\nInexact DC Algorithms in Hilbert Spaces with Applications to PDE-Constrained Optimization\n\nP. D. Khanh, V. V. H. Khoa, B. S. Mordukhovich, D. B. Tran, and 1 more author\n\narXiv preprint arXiv:2601.06622, 2026\n    \n    `@article{khanh2026inexact,\n      title = {Inexact DC Algorithms in Hilbert Spaces with Applications to PDE-Constrained Optimization},\n      author = {Khanh, P. D. and Khoa, V. V. H. and Mordukhovich, B. S. and Tran, D. B. and Vo, N. V.},\n      journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.06622},\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/learning-to-shuffle-block-reshuffling-and-reversal-schemes-for-stochastic/wrk_f80c8587507af3b6bf0e8386b3791165)\nciteturn36240search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic OptimizationarXiv · 2026Presentation Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic OptimizationCoRR · 2026\n\n# Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization\n\nLam M. Nguyen, Dzung T. Phan, Jayant Kalagnanam\n\narXiv 2026\n\n## Versions\n\nLearning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic OptimizationarXiv · 2026PresentationLearning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic OptimizationCoRR · 2026\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThe paper introduces an LLM-guided program evolution approach to discover optimal data-shuffling strategies for without-replacement Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD). By analyzing the evolved algorithm, the authors identify two powerful structural mechanisms—block reshuffling and paired reversal—that provably reduce gradient variance and order sensitivity, outperforming standard random reshuffling across multiple convex and non-convex benchmarks.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nWhen training machine learning models, we almost always process data \"without replacement\" by shuffling the dataset at the start of each epoch. Common classical strategies include Random Reshuffling (RR), Shuffle-Once (SO), and Incremental Gradient (IG).\n\nWhile empirical evidence and recent unified analyses show that RR generally performs best, existing optimization theory largely treats these data permutations as completely unstructured objects. Consequently, there has been little theoretical or practical guidance on how to design better data-ordering schemes that can beat standard random reshuffling. This paper bridges that gap by combining automated algorithm discovery with new theoretical insights to find structured shuffling rules that improve optimization speed and stability.\n\n## Methods\n\nThe authors first deploy an offline pipeline called OpenEvolve (powered by a large language model) to search the space of deterministic Python functions that generate data permutations based on epoch-level training feedback (such as loss statistics).\n\nFrom the high-performing programs discovered by the LLM, they formalize an algorithm called Adaptive Block Reshuffling with Periodic Transforms (APR). APR uses a \"loss-ratio gating\" mechanism to adaptively switch between different shuffling regimes depending on training progress:\n\n  * Strong improvement: Applies block reshuffling with small blocks and periodic sequence reversal.\n  * Mild improvement: Applies block reshuffling with larger blocks to introduce more randomness.\n  * No improvement: Falls back to full random reshuffling with an optional even-odd interleaving transform.\n\nThe authors isolate and theoretically analyze the two core structural pillars of this approach:\n\n  1. Block Reshuffling: Partitions the dataset into consecutive blocks, randomly permutes the blocks, but preserves the data order within each block.\n  2. Paired Reversal: Averages the updates of a permutation and its exact reverse sequence to introduce symmetry.\n\n## Datasets\n\nThe authors evaluated the shuffling strategies across a diverse collection of standard benchmark datasets:\n\n  * Convex Classification: `a9a`, `breast_cancer`, and `digits` (trained using linear logistic regression models).\n  * Convex Regression: `California Housing`, `Boston Housing`, and `Diabetes` from `sklearn.datasets` (trained using linear regression with mean squared error loss).\n  * Non-Convex Classification: `MNIST` and `FashionMNIST` (trained using a fully connected multilayer perceptron with ReLU activations and the Adam optimizer).\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * The LLM as an Offline Discovery Tool: Instead of deploying an LLM dynamically during training (which would introduce massive computational overhead), the LLM is used purely offline to explore the algorithmic design space. The resulting APR algorithm is explicit, deterministic, and adds zero runtime overhead.\n  * Block Reshuffling as Variance Reduction: From a statistical perspective, block reshuffling acts like sampling block-averaged gradients without replacement rather than individual gradients. The authors prove that this strictly reduces prefix-gradient variance constants compared to standard random reshuffling, provided the gradients within a block possess local coherence.\n  * Paired Reversal Cancels Order Sensitivity: Sequential updates within an epoch introduce drift. By symmetrizing the epoch map (averaging a permutation and its reverse), paired reversal completely cancels out the leading order-dependent second-order term. This reduces the optimization's sensitivity to data ordering from quadratic ($\\mathcal{O}(\\gamma^2)$) to cubic ($\\mathcal{O}(\\gamma^3)$) relative to the learning rate $\\gamma$, acting as a powerful stabilizer.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Lower Training Loss: The evolved APR strategy consistently achieved lower best-so-far training loss compared to IG, SO, and RR across all convex classification and regression tasks.\n  * Compatibility with Adaptive Optimizers: Even when paired with the Adam optimizer on non-convex tasks like FashionMNIST, APR remained highly competitive and often improved the final training loss.\n  * Enhanced Stability: As shown in the training dynamics, the gains are particularly prominent under constant learning rates where order-dependent effects are strongest. APR also demonstrated visibly tighter confidence bands, confirming its theoretical variance-reduction and stability benefits.\n\nExtracted figure from page 8 of Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\nWhile the authors provided rigorous independent proofs for the benefits of block reshuffling and paired reversal, they did not theoretically analyze the full, complex adaptive logic of the entire APR algorithm as a single unified system. The transitions between the different block sizes and sequence transforms are currently guided by heuristic thresholds discovered by the LLM. The authors suggest that future work could focus on developing a more comprehensive convergence theory that natively incorporates these adaptive, data-dependent permutation selections.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Lam M. Nguyen (https://dblp.org/pid/181/1428.html)\nciteturn36240search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; CoRR abs/2602.06909 (2026) ... Learning to Shuffle: Block Reshuffling and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization.\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * 