\nNguyen, Dzung Phan, Aviad Sela, Carolina Spina, Dharmashankar Subramanian,\nAbel Valente, Long Vu, Dakuo Wang, Segev Wasserkrug, Ritesh Yadav, Nianjun Zhou\nThe 36th AAAI Conference on Artiﬁcial Intelligence (AAAI 2022), AI for Decision\nOptimization, AI4DO, 2022\n[1]\nEnsuring the Quality of Optimization Solutions in Data Generated Optimization Models.\nSegev Wasserkrug, Orit Davidovith, Evgeny Shindin, Dharmashankar Subramanian,\nParikshit Ram, Pavankumar Murali, Dzung Phan, Nianjun Zhou, Lam M. Nguyen\nThe 30th International Joint Conference on Artiﬁcial Intelligence (IJCAI 2021), Data\nScience Meets Optimisation, DSO@IJCAI2021, 2021\nPREPRINTS\n[17]\nLearning to Shufﬂe: Block Reshufﬂing and Reversal Schemes for Stochastic Optimization.\nLam M. Nguyen, Dzung T. Phan, Jayant Kalagnanam\nTechnical report, arXiv preprint, 2026\n[16]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/diversity-aware-reverse-kullback-leibler-divergence-for-large-language-model/wrk_acf6fc37cc51ccf77e1eec320cbbf606)\nciteturn36240search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation ... arXiv 2026 ... Reverse Kullback-Leibler (RKL) divergence is highly effective for distilling large language models because it focuses on dominant modes, but it suffers from a structural flaw that makes student models overconfident and reduces output diversity.\n\n# Diversity-Aware Reverse Kullback-Leibler Divergence for Large Language Model Distillation\n\nHoang-Chau Luong, Dat Ba Tran, Lingwei Chen\n\narXiv 2026\n\n## TL;DR\n\nReverse Kullback-Leibler (RKL) divergence is highly effective for distilling large language models because it focuses on dominant modes, but it suffers from a structural flaw that makes student models overconfident and reduces output diversity. To fix this, the authors propose Diversity-Aware RKL (DRKL), which decouples target and non-target gradients to eliminate overconfidence while strengthening tail-end distribution alignment. DRKL consistently outperforms existing distillation methods across multiple benchmarks, achieving a superior balance of fidelity and diversity.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nKnowledge distillation (KD) compresses massive \"teacher\" LLMs into smaller, deployment-friendly \"student\" models by minimizing the difference between their output probability distributions. Historically, Forward KL (FKL) was the standard objective, but recent LLM distillation favors Reverse KL (RKL) due to its superior empirical performance. RKL exhibits \"mode-seeking\" behavior, focusing on high-probability tokens rather than trying to match the teacher's massive, heavy-tailed vocabulary distribution—a task that easily overwhelms capacity-limited student models.\n\nHowever, RKL has a major downside: it systematically reduces the entropy of the student's distribution, leading to overconfident predictions and a severe drop in output diversity. Students trained with RKL often become even more confident than their teachers, harming generation quality. This paper investigates why RKL causes this overconfidence and introduces a solution to restore output diversity without sacrificing RKL's optimization advantages.\n\n## Methods\n\nThe authors perform a target/non-target gradient decomposition of the RKL objective. They discover two key issues:\n\n  1. Confidence Amplification: The gradient from non-target classes with respect to the target logit is consistently negative, meaning it continuously pushes the target logit upward even after the student's target probability matches the teacher's.\n  2. The Non-Target Shortcut: In standard RKL, the non-target loss is scaled by $(1 - q_m)$, where $q_m$ is the student's target probability. This allows the model to minimize non-target loss simply by maximizing target confidence ($q_m \\rightarrow 1$), leading to poor alignment of the remaining vocabulary tail.\n\nTo solve this, they introduce Diversity-Aware RKL (DRKL). DRKL removes the $(1 - q_m)$ scaling factor from the non-target term and replaces it with a fixed hyperparameter $\\gamma$:\n\n$$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{DRKL}} = D_{\\text{KL}}(\\tilde{q}_m \\parallel \\tilde{p}_m) + \\gamma D_{\\text{KL}}(\\hat{q} \\parallel \\hat{p})$$\n\nThis simple modification completely eliminates the non-target gradient's upward pressure on the target logit and ensures robust, continuous supervision over non-target classes.\n\n## Datasets\n\nThe models were trained on the instruction-response dataset derived from databricks-dolly-15k (14k training samples, 500 validation, 500 test).\n\nEvaluation was conducted across five prominent instruction-following benchmarks:\n\n  * Dolly validation set / Dolly Eval\n  * Self-Instruct\n  * Vicuna Eval\n  * Super-Natural Instructions (Super-NI)\n  * Unnatural Instructions (UnNI)\n\n## Key Insights and Ideas\n\n  * RKL's Advantage is Structural: RKL outperforms FKL in LLMs because it restricts active optimization to a tiny fraction of the vocabulary ($|A_C| \\ll V$), making the alignment problem manageable for smaller student models under severe capacity mismatch.\n  * The Overconfidence Flaw: RKL's overconfidence isn't just a side effect of mode-seeking; it's driven by a mathematical quirk where misaligned non-target classes exert a persistent upward force on the target logit.\n  * Decoupling is Key: By removing the dynamic target-probability weight from the non-target loss component, DRKL stops the target logit from blowing up while forcing the student to actually learn the \"dark knowledge\" hidden in the teacher's non-target distribution.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Consistently Higher Fidelity: DRKL ranks first in average performance across all benchmarks for both GPT-2 and OPT model families. ---------------------------